WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.488
"I’ve decided to enjoy this drive," Anne said to Marilla on the way to Mrs. Spencer's house.

2
00:00:07.489 --> 00:00:12.302
"I've learned you can nearly always enjoy something if you try hard.

3
00:00:12.303 --> 00:00:15.117
I’m not going to think about going back to the orphanage.

4
00:00:15.118 --> 00:00:17.695
I’m just going to think about the drive.

5
00:00:17.696 --> 00:00:21.880
Oh look, there’s a wild rose!" she went on.

6
00:00:21.881 --> 00:00:26.372
"Isn’t it lovely? Wouldn’t it be nice if roses could talk?

7
00:00:26.373 --> 00:00:30.124
And isn’t pink the most bewitching color in the world?

8
00:00:30.125 --> 00:00:35.836
I love it, but I can’t wear it. Redheaded people can’t wear pink.

9
00:00:35.837 --> 00:00:39.982
Do you know anybody whose hair color changed when they grew up?"

10
00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:46.675
"No," said Marilla wearily. "And I don’t think your hair color will change either."

11
00:00:46.676 --> 00:00:52.989
"Well, that’s another hope gone. My life is a graveyard of buried hopes.

12
00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.427
That’s a sentence I read in a book once.

13
00:00:55.428 --> 00:00:58.860
I say it to comfort myself when I’m disappointed."

14
00:00:58.861 --> 00:01:02.755
"How does it comfort you?" asked Marilla, surprised.

15
00:01:02.756 --> 00:01:09.404
"Why, because it sounds so nice and romantic, as if I were a heroine in a book."

16
00:01:09.405 --> 00:01:14.840
"If you’re going to keep talking, you should tell me something about yourself," said Marilla.

17
00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:19.683
"Can I make it up?" Anne asked. "It’ll be much more interesting."

18
00:01:19.684 --> 00:01:23.804
"No, I don’t want any stories. Just stick to the facts.

19
00:01:23.805 --> 00:01:28.628
Where were you born and how old are you?" asked Marilla sternly.

20
00:01:28.629 --> 00:01:34.455
"I was eleven last March," said Anne, "and I was born in Nova Scotia.

21
00:01:34.456 --> 00:01:37.670
My father and mother were both high school teachers.

22
00:01:37.671 --> 00:01:43.200
They were very poor and lived in a tiny yellow house. I was born in that house.

23
00:01:43.201 --> 00:01:50.516
Mrs. Thomas said I was an ugly baby, scrawny and thin, but that my mother thought I was perfectly beautiful.

24
00:01:50.517 --> 00:01:53.856
She died of fever when I was only three months old.

25
00:01:53.857 --> 00:01:57.726
And soon after, my father died from fever too.

26
00:01:57.727 --> 00:02:01.142
"So I was an orphan and nobody knew what to do with me.

27
00:02:01.143 --> 00:02:06.452
Father and Mother had both come from places far away and had no relatives around.

28
00:02:06.453 --> 00:02:12.168
Finally Mrs. Thomas took me in, even though she was poor and her husband was a drunk.

29
00:02:12.169 --> 00:02:15.122
I lived with them until I was eight years old.

30
00:02:15.123 --> 00:02:18.912
I helped look after the children, and it was hard work.

31
00:02:18.913 --> 00:02:22.753
"Then Mr. Thomas was killed when he fell under a train.

32
00:02:22.754 --> 00:02:26.350
After that I lived with the Hammonds, who had eight children.

33
00:02:26.351 --> 00:02:32.663
She had twins three times! I like babies, but twins three times is too much!

34
00:02:32.664 --> 00:02:35.511
I got so tired carrying them around.

35
00:02:35.512 --> 00:02:39.927
I lived with the Hammonds for two years, and then Mr. Hammond died.

36
00:02:39.928 --> 00:02:44.660
I had to go to the orphanage because the Hammonds didn’t want me any longer either.

37
00:02:44.661 --> 00:02:47.155
It was too crowded in their house."

38
00:02:47.156 --> 00:02:50.447
"Did you ever go to school?" Marilla asked.

39
00:02:50.448 --> 00:02:57.930
"A little bit," answered Anne. "I can read well, and I know lots of poetry! Don’t you just love poetry?"

40
00:02:57.931 --> 00:03:04.955
"Were those women, Mrs. Thomas and Mrs. Hammond, good to you?" asked Marilla, looking at Anne carefully.

41
00:03:04.956 --> 00:03:10.404
"Uh . . . um . . . ," stammered Anne, blushing with embarrassment.

42
00:03:10.405 --> 00:03:14.792
"They tried to be good to me. I’m sure they meant to be good."

43
00:03:14.793 --> 00:03:22.703
Marilla asked no more questions. She suddenly felt pity for Anne, who hadn’t had much love in her short life.

44
00:03:22.704 --> 00:03:25.712
Soon they arrived at Mrs. Spencer’s house.

45
00:03:25.713 --> 00:03:32.717
"Well," Mrs. Spencer exclaimed, "you’re the last folks I expected! How are you, Anne?"

46
00:03:32.718 --> 00:03:36.148
"All right," said Anne without smiling.

47
00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:41.599
"Mrs. Spencer," began Marilla. "I’m sorry, but there’s been a mistake.

48
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.016
We told the orphanage we wanted a boy."

49
00:03:45.017 --> 00:03:51.138
"Oh!" Mrs. Spencer said. "That’s too bad. I’m very sorry."

50
00:03:51.139 --> 00:03:55.999
"I’m sorry too," said Marilla. "Can we send the child back?

51
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.602
The orphanage will take her, won’t they?"

52
00:03:58.603 --> 00:04:02.577
"I suppose so," said Mrs. Spencer thoughtfully.

53
00:04:02.578 --> 00:04:06.347
"But wait a minute. Mrs. Blewett was here yesterday.

54
00:04:06.348 --> 00:04:09.114
She told me she would like a little girl.

55
00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:12.429
Marilla was not very pleased to hear this.

56
00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:17.871
Mrs. Blewett had a terrible temper and did not treat servant girls well.

57
00:04:17.872 --> 00:04:21.974
"Look, there's Mrs. Blewett now!" said Mrs. Spencer.

58
00:04:21.975 --> 00:04:25.884
A thin-faced, frowning woman came over to the house.

59
00:04:25.885 --> 00:04:29.370
"How lucky! She can take Anne now!"

60
00:04:29.371 --> 00:04:33.207
Mrs. Spencer explained the mistake to Mrs. Blewett.

61
00:04:33.208 --> 00:04:36.562
Mrs. Blewett looked Anne over from head to toe.

62
00:04:36.563 --> 00:04:40.119
"How old are you and what’s your name?" she demanded.

63
00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:45.038
"My name is Anne Shirley, and I’m eleven years old," Anne replied.

64
00:04:45.039 --> 00:04:49.291
"You’re too thin, but you’re wiry," snapped Mrs. Blewett.

65
00:04:49.292 --> 00:04:53.699
"The wiry ones are the best. You’ll be a good girl and work hard.

66
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:59.972
All right, Miss Cuthbert," she said to Marilla. "I’ll take her home now."

67
00:04:59.973 --> 00:05:07.781
"I don’t know," Marilla said slowly. "Matthew and I haven’t decided what to do yet.

68
00:05:07.782 --> 00:05:12.825
If we decide not to keep Anne, I’ll send her over tomorrow."

69
00:05:12.826 --> 00:05:17.001
"All right," said Mrs. Blewett ungraciously.

70
00:05:17.002 --> 00:05:21.576
At Marilla's words Anne’s eyes grew bright like stars.

71
00:05:21.577 --> 00:05:26.211
"Can I really stay at Green Gables?" she whispered to Marilla.

72
00:05:26.212 --> 00:05:32.099
"We’ll decide tonight," said Marilla crossly. "But you heard me.

73
00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.813
You might go to Mrs. Blewett. She needs you more than Matthew and I do."

74
00:05:36.814 --> 00:05:43.248
"Oh, send me to the orphanage instead! Mrs. Blewett looks like a monster!" said Anne.

75
00:05:43.249 --> 00:05:48.011
"Anne Shirley! Don’t talk like that!" Marilla scolded.

76
00:05:48.012 --> 00:05:51.333
"Go and sit quietly and be a good girl!"

77
00:05:51.334 --> 00:05:55.736
"I’ll be anything you want, if you will keep me," pleaded Anne.

78
00:05:55.737 --> 00:06:01.805
Later Marilla told Matthew about Anne’s history and the trip to Mrs. Spencer’s house.

79
00:06:01.806 --> 00:06:05.618
"I wouldn’t give a dog to Mrs. Blewett!" said Matthew.

80
00:06:05.619 --> 00:06:14.061
"I don’t like Mrs. Blewett's style either," admitted Marilla, "Since you want to keep the girl, I suppose she can stay.

81
00:06:14.062 --> 00:06:17.546
I’ve never brought up a child, especially a girl.

82
00:06:17.547 --> 00:06:21.867
I’ll probably make a mess of it, but I’ll do my best."

83
00:06:21.868 --> 00:06:29.900
Matthew’s shy face glowed with delight. "Marilla, I hoped you would change your mind. She’s such an interesting little thing."

84
00:06:29.901 --> 00:06:34.307
"Yes, well, don’t interfere, Matthew," Marilla said.

85
00:06:34.308 --> 00:06:38.810
"I won’t tell Anne tonight because she’ll be so excited she won’t sleep.

86
00:06:38.811 --> 00:06:44.913
An old maid might not know much about raising children, but neither does an old bachelor!

87
00:06:44.914 --> 00:06:54.527
Goodness knows what will come of it!"

